5 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120276 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120276 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1, 0, 0)$ where $t = 1$, given that\n\n$T(1) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i + j), N(1) = k$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120276 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "857f2717-334c-550e-bce1-cd3a3ad47f82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120298 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = π$, given that\n\n$r(t) = ⟨t, \\cos t, \\sin t⟩, T(π) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1, 0,-1⟩, N(π) = ⟨0, 1, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "091c6a72-5e82-5c94-a2c2-b3675bcf57e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120567 #edb/ref 1061 10274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ed71b2f-4ce7-54f0-b2bc-730ad0cfda29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120568 #edb/ref 1061 10275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44c831c7-9b75-5efc-8fa9-ddb3c7b148d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120569 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120569 #edb/ref 1061 10278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04b3287e-7857-5988-8e8d-faaa11fc45a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120570 #edb/ref 1061 10279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36ec013b-6152-55b4-abd3-ca3407f4ed05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120571 #edb/ref 1061 10282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120571 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b8cab73-ce4b-5307-9379-d3f0465cede8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120571 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120997 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function $$\\mathbf f (t)= \\big\\langle2 \\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\big\\rangle.$$ At the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$ the function, unit tangent, and the principal normal are given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩ \\\\ T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince the unit tangent $\\mathbf T$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N$ are not collinear, they define a *unique* plane, called the **osculating plane.** In general, the osculating plane to a curve at a point $P$ is the plane of \"greatest contact\" with the curve at $P.$\n\n![](images/0a/0af5fd8c4fce63bbc15fe35d40e20602fbd1420bcd23f875368ddcb76755cb79.png)\n\nSince the osculating plane contains the unit tangent vector and the principal normal vector, we can write the equation of the osculating plane in parametric form as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+s\\,T(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+u\\,N(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨6,\\,-6\\sqrt{3},\\,5\\pi ⟩+\\frac{s}{5}⟨-\\sqrt{15},\\,-\\sqrt{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩+\\frac{u}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}$$ where $s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, for a curve $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ the osculating plane at the point $t=t_0$ has the parametric equation $$\\mathbf R(s,u) = \\mathbf r \\left( t_0 \\right) + s \\, \\mathbf T \\left( t_0 \\right) + u \\, \\mathbf N \\left( t_0 \\right), \\qquad s, u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),$$ where $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and $\\mathbf{N}(t_0)$ are the unit tangent and the principal normal to the curve at $t=t_0,$ respectively.\n\n**Note:** A straight line does not have a principal normal, and consequently it does not have an osculating plane either." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120997 #edb/ref 1065 2370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120997 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "47992101-e151-57a2-9a0f-aa58c4db168f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120997 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120998 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function $\\mathbf f(t).$ Given a point $P$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf p,$ we can find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane at $P$ using the following three steps.\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we calculate the unit tangent $\\mathbf T(t_0)$ and the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t_0).$\n- **Step 2:** Next, we calculate the cross product $\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf T(t_0) \\times \\mathbf N(t_0).$ Recall that $\\mathbf{n}$ will be perpendicular to the plane containing both the tangent $\\mathbf{T}(t_0)$ and normal $\\mathbf{N}(t_0).$\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write down the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane: $$\\big( \\langle x, y, z \\rangle - \\mathbf{p} \\big) \\cdot \\mathbf n = 0$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120998 #edb/ref 1065 2371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120998 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c56b88c4-34db-5020-8461-5d9295e3dc51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120998 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375825 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f4a870e-ec48-5855-9ff2-1d02c3e15a46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375825 #edb/ref 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(0,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375826 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "187786e6-deb0-5d64-8f2a-517c86ffee30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375827 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375827 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375827 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38c1e0ab-2828-5e9c-9df8-5e2c181ea0fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375828 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375829 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s,u)=⟨-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375829 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375829 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3bd6cff-b279-5c03-a2c3-437153fecca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375830 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e5e799c3-9581-586b-8d38-bfd28a7079b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375831 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "267277fb-aa68-5bfb-abab-4bd34d2544b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375853 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,2)$ where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\nT(\\pi )=i,\\,N(\\pi )=-k.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bbe6880-9115-5b6f-a9bb-cfd1553b146d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375853 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,2)$ is $p=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =i\\times (-k) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi )=j=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(1)+z(0) & =0(0)+1(1)+2(0) \\\\ 0+y+0 & =0+1+0 \\\\ y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375854 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "38507475-0514-5b9b-b6cc-a25300e0334e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375855 #edb/ref 1080 "y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cce7a57-ec50-5b7d-a10d-d12d340f7ebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375856 #edb/ref 1080 "z-2=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0dd603f8-dae9-5232-88ef-942f6c34a5d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375857 #edb/ref 1080 "y+z-2=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375857 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e673e372-7f75-50af-8159-f2472454cfed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375858 #edb/ref 1080 "x+y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375858 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98881dce-2ee9-5276-ac33-8fcb4aca837c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375859 #edb/ref 1080 "y-z=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375859 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375859 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a92762f-efaa-559b-abe8-23eb75919596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375860 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "569b65f9-8938-5c3e-b130-07826f3d58f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375860 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0,2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(-1)+z(0) & =0(0)+2(-1)+(-1)(0) \\\\ 0-y+0 & =0-2+0 \\\\ y-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375861 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "91d9bf77-7863-573d-8373-7f2526605345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375862 #edb/ref 1080 "y-2=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375862 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375862 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1cecf739-72ba-5487-96f6-0db04f45b2ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375863 #edb/ref 1080 "x-y-z+1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375863 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375863 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5405c60-125e-50c7-b4d5-cad7b7311891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375864 #edb/ref 1080 "x+y+z-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186